{{!
    @template block_compass/tier2

    The tier 2 ghost card, mounted as a React component (ADR-006, phase R1).

    The react section carries a JSON object naming the component and its props,
    followed by the content shown until React takes over - and shown for good if
    it never does, since a failed import leaves the element untouched with only a
    console message. That fallback is therefore deliberately NOT a button: it
    states the count, which is true, and offers no action, because there would be
    nothing behind it. Only the mounted component renders the call to action, so a
    step asserting the call to action is asserting that React mounted.

    The props arrive already JSON-encoded, in one value, through a triple stash.
    That is not a style choice and the obvious alternative is broken: wrapping each
    string in the quote helper corrupts any value containing two or three
    consecutive curly braces. The quote helper JSON-encodes and then neutralises
    such a run with a Mustache delimiter-change directive, which only comes out
    right if something downstream re-parses the result as a template - and nothing
    does here, in either engine, so the directive text itself lands in the props.
    Measured on m502 in phase R1 with a label holding a doubled-brace pair around a
    word: it reached the component as the escape markers, while the fallback beside
    it, a plain double stash, showed the original. No console error, no JSON syntax
    error, nothing to see. JSON.stringify in the caller is the whole fix. (The
    offending sequence is described rather than written because a Mustache comment
    closes at the first pair of closing braces, which would truncate this docblock.)

    The stash is triple because nothing between here and React is an HTML context:
    the value is JSON, carried in a data attribute, parsed back with JSON.parse and
    rendered as a React text node, which escapes for itself. A double stash would
    reach the reader as literal entities. Mustache does not re-scan an interpolated
    value, so braces inside the JSON stay inert.

    Rendered from the browser by block_compass/attention, so the section is
    processed by the client-side Mustache engine, which implements the same helper
    as the PHP one (lib/amd/src/local/templates/renderer.js).

    Context variables required for this template:
    * count - the number of courses not shown in tier 1, for the fallback.
    * text - what is counted, already translated, for the fallback.
    * props - the component's props, already JSON-encoded by the caller: an object
      with count, text, cta and kind, the last being tier2, new or favourites,
      which decides the chip preselected when tier 3 opens.

    Example context (json):
    {
        "count": 87,
        "text": "other courses & more",
        "props": "{\"count\": 87, \"text\": \"other courses & more\", \"cta\": \"Explore all\", \"kind\": \"tier2\"}"
    }
}}
{{#react}}
{
    "component": "@moodle/lms/block_compass/Ghost",
    "props": {{{props}}},
    "class": "compass-ghost-mount"
}
<div class="compass-ghost compass-ghost-fallback card h-100 text-center w-100">
    <span class="card-body d-flex flex-column justify-content-center">
        <span class="compass-ghost-count">+{{count}}</span>
        <span class="compass-ghost-text small text-muted">{{text}}</span>
    </span>
</div>
{{/react}}
